course: dictionary
lesson: 9716
title: Summarising the main points
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To summarise means to give the main points of something in a few words, leaving out the details. Think of it as telling the important bits, not every bit. «Summarising both sides fairly before a vote shows you understood them.» Here, summarising means you say the main idea of each side in a few words.
explain: At school you may be asked to «summarise the story in three sentences», «summarise the paragraph» or «summarise your results». A good summary keeps the key ideas and uses your own words, not copied sentences.
explain: To check your summary, ask yourself: what is the most important thing here? If a detail is not needed to understand the main idea, leave it out.
fact: To summarise means to give the main points in a few words.
fact: A summary leaves out the details and keeps the key ideas.
fact: «Summarise in one sentence» means you use just one sentence for the main points.
fact: A good summary uses your own words, not copied ones.
fact: «Summarising both sides fairly» means you give the main points of each side, not just one side.
exercise: To summarise a story means to: | Draw a picture of the best part | *Give the main points in a few words | Tell every single detail, word for word | Read it out loud twice
exercise: «Summarise the paragraph» asks you to: | Copy the whole paragraph out again | Count how many words it has | *Give its main points in your own words | Skip it and go on
exercise: «Summarising both sides fairly before a vote shows you understood them.» What does summarising mean here? | *Giving the main points of each side in a few words | Copying every word from both sides | Shouting the loudest argument | Making up a new story about the vote
test: Which sentence is a good summary of a story about a dog who finds his way home? | The dog barked at a cat, a bird and a bike, and then he barked again | *A lost dog finds his way home. | The dog was brown, had a red collar, and ate his dinner by the big oak tree on Tuesday
test: «Summarise your results in one sentence.» Which answer does this ask for? | Three pages about the experiment | *One sentence with the main result | A drawing of the lab | A list of every number you measured
exam: Mia has read a long page about rainforests. Which is the best way to summarise it? | Copy the first line and stop | *Write a few sentences with the main ideas in your own words | Write a poem about trees | Write down every fact in the order it appears
